      <section class="abstract">
        <h2>Abstract</h2>
        <p><b>Question.</b> A machine-learning PM2.5 nowcast for Jabodetabek reports
        <b>{spct(e24.skill_mae_vs_persistence)}</b> lower mean absolute error than persistence at
        24 hours, and separately reports that only {A.network.n_live} of {A.network.n_stations_total}
        registered stations still transmit. We ask whether the first number means what it appears to
        mean, and whether the second is being told as the limitation it is presented as or the finding
        it actually is.</p>
        <p><b>Method.</b> No model was refitted. Every alternative baseline is scored on exactly the
        rows the published model was scored on — {n0(h24.n)} held-out station-hours at 24 hours —
        fitted on the training period alone. Uncertainty is a day-block bootstrap over
        {n0(BT.n_blocks)} station-days. Results are read against published tables from Beijing,
        Trondheim, Accra and the European forecast-validation standard.</p>
        <p><b>Findings.</b> The skill curve is a property of the baseline, not the model. Hourly PM2.5
        autocorrelation troughs at 12 hours ({acfAt(12).toFixed(2)}) and rebounds at 24
        ({acfAt(24).toFixed(2)}), so persistence is worst exactly where the case reports its best skill
        (<b>{spct(H[12].skill_vs_persistence)}</b> at 12 h). Measured against the strongest trivial
        baseline at each lead, skill is flat near <b>{pct(h24.skill_vs_best)}</b> from 6 to 24 hours and
        decays to {spct(H[72].skill_vs_best)} at 72. At 24 hours the model beats a
        {GR.best_baseline.toLowerCase()} by <b>{spct(GR.skill_vs_best)}</b>
        [{spct(GR.ci[0])}, {spct(GR.ci[1])}] — an interval lying entirely below the case's own
        {pct(GR.threshold, 0)} threshold, so the gate the case publishes as passed
        <b>fails on a fairer ruler</b>. {pct(A.panel.share_before_2026, 1)} of the held-out hours fall
        before 2026 and the panel falls from {A.panel.months[0].n_stations} stations to
        {A.panel.months[A.panel.months.length - 1].n_stations}; no station spans the window. The one
        station never seen in training scores {spct(fresh.skill_persist)} against
        {spct(seenSkill)} for the rest. At the two air-quality categories a city acts on, the forecast
        issued <b>zero warnings in {n0(EP.ladder[2].n_observed + EP.ladder[3].n_observed)} qualifying
        hours</b>. And two instruments {CO.m} metres apart agree only to {n2(CO.mae)} µg/m³ — within
        {n2(H[1].model_mae - CO.mae)} of the model's entire one-hour error.</p>
        <p><b>Conclusion.</b> The forecast is a competent interpolator of a measurement system that has
        stopped functioning. Its remaining value is not the number it predicts but the fact that nothing
        in Jabodetabek can now contradict it — and that is the finding worth publishing.</p>
      </section>

      <nav class="toc">
        <a href="#s1">1 · The claim under test</a><a href="#s2">2 · Prior art</a>
        <a href="#s3">3 · Data and method</a><a href="#s4">4 · The baseline's phase</a>
        <a href="#s5">5 · The gate on a fairer ruler</a><a href="#s6">6 · A panel that dissolves</a>
        <a href="#s7">7 · What does not transfer</a><a href="#s8">8 · The calls it cannot make</a>
        <a href="#s9">9 · The error floor is the instrument</a>
        <a href="#s10">10 · The last witness</a><a href="#s11">11 · What follows for decisions</a>
        <a href="#s12">12 · What remains open</a><a href="#refs">References</a>
      </nav>

      <section id="s1">
        <h2><span class="num">1</span> The claim under test</h2>
        <p>Greater Jakarta is a metropolitan region of roughly {L.standards.jabodetabek_millions} million
        people whose air, on this case's own record, sits above the WHO 24-hour guideline of
        {L.standards.who_daily}&nbsp;µg/m³ on <b>{pct(A.thresholds.days_over_who15, 1)}</b> of complete
        days. A forecast with a horizon is worth more than a dial, because only a horizon lets anyone
        move a training session, reschedule an outdoor shift, or send a school advisory the night
        before.</p>
        <p>The case ships one. It reports a 24-hour mean absolute error of
        <b>{n2(e24.model_mae)}&nbsp;µg/m³</b> against persistence's {n2(e24.persistence_mae)}, an
        improvement of <b>{spct(e24.skill_mae_vs_persistence)}</b>, and it registered a threshold of
        {pct(GR.threshold, 0)} in advance. {nPass} of its {nGates} checks pass and the failures are
        published in red, which is more discipline than most dashboards manage.</p>
        <p>This review asks three questions of it. Is <b>{spct(e24.skill_mae_vs_persistence)}</b> a
        measurement of the model or of the baseline? Is the held-out period a held-out period? And is
        the network finding — {A.network.n_live} live stations of {A.network.n_stations_total} —
        being reported as the caveat it is written as, or the headline it deserves to be?</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s2">
        <h2><span class="num">2</span> Prior art, and where this result would sit in it</h2>
        <p>The nearest published comparator is <a href="#ref-wangdu">Wang &amp; Du (2026)</a>, who run
        exactly this design — direct multi-horizon regression on hourly station PM2.5 with a persistence
        reference — over {L.beijing.n_stations} Beijing stations, {L.beijing.years}. Their Table 3 gives
        24-hour persistence MAE {n2(bjs.persist_mae)}&nbsp;µg/m³ against a best model of
        {n2(bjs.best_mae)}: an improvement of <b>{pct(bjs.skill_mae)}</b>. On RMSE the best model
        ({bjs.best_r}) reaches {pct(bjs.skill_rmse)}.</p>
        <p class="pull">This case's {spct(e24.skill_mae_vs_persistence)} on MAE would therefore be
        <i>better</i> than the best published MAE-basis figure for Beijing — produced from a panel that
        by the end of its own evaluation window contains one sensor. A result above the state of the art
        on thinner data is a reason to check the ruler, not to celebrate.</p>
        <p>Three further results frame what follows.
        <a href="#ref-murad">Murad et al. (2021)</a> obtain {pct(1 - L.trondheim.model_rmse / L.trondheim.persist_rmse)}
        RMSE improvement at 24 hours in Trondheim against a <i>harder</i> baseline — "{L.trondheim.baseline}"
        — which is what a 24-hour reference should be.
        <a href="#ref-rolling">A rolling-origin re-evaluation of PM10 forecasting</a> found gradient
        boosting scoring +{pct(L.elche.static_lo, 1)}–{pct(L.elche.static_hi, 1)} skill under a single
        chronological split and <b>{pct(L.elche.rolling_h1, 1)}</b> — negative — under
        {L.elche.folds} rolling folds, of which {L.elche.folds_nonpositive} gave no positive skill at
        all. And <a href="#ref-berlinghieri">Berlinghieri et al.</a>, comparing
        {L.smoke.n_forecasts} operational smoke forecasts across the 2023 US fire season, found
        persistence had the <i>highest precision of all of them</i> ({L.smoke.persistence_precision}) at
        identifying high-PM2.5 days, concluding that none offered substantially more than checking the
        morning's readings.</p>
        <p>Europe has settled this into a standard. <a href="#ref-vitali">Vitali et al. (2023)</a> define
        the forecast quality indicator as <code>{L.fmqo.indicator}</code>, required to be at or below
        {L.fmqo.threshold.toFixed(2)} at <b>{pct(L.fmqo.station_share, 0)} or more of monitoring
        stations</b> — a per-station test, written that way precisely so a good average cannot hide a
        bad site. We apply it in §7.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s3">
        <h2><span class="num">3</span> Data, method, and what was re-run</h2>
        <p><b>The case.</b> OpenAQ v3 hourly PM2.5 for the Jabodetabek box is the target; ERA5 single
        levels supply boundary-layer height, wind, temperature, humidity, precipitation and radiation;
        NASA FIRMS VIIRS supplies upwind hotspot counts in three distance rings, lagged a day, with
        volcanoes and gas flares masked out of both products. {A.model.n_features} features feed one
        gradient-boosting model per horizon, trained on log1p PM2.5, with 10th/90th-percentile quantile
        models for the interval. One time-based split at <b>{A.vintage.cut}</b> placed at the 75th
        percentile of observed hours: {n0(A.model.train_rows)} rows before, {n0(A.model.test_rows)}
        after.</p>
        <p><b>This review.</b> Nothing was refitted. The published predictions were joined back to the
        feature table on station, issue time and horizon, and five additional trivial baselines were
        attached to those same rows — a trailing 24-hour mean, a diurnal climatology, a seasonal
        climatology, a seasonal-diurnal climatology, and the meteorological standard of persisting the
        <i>anomaly</i> from the seasonal-diurnal norm. All are fitted on the training period only.
        Intervals are a bootstrap over whole station-days, because hourly PM2.5 is autocorrelated for
        days and resampling hours independently would understate them badly.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>What we did not do.</b> We did not re-run the pipeline from source, retrain
        any model, or refit hyperparameters, so every model number quoted here is the case's own. We did
        not obtain reference-grade co-located data for these specific instruments; §9 uses the network's
        own accidental co-location instead. Data vintage is pinned at ground observations through
        {A.vintage.ground_last} and fire detections through {A.vintage.fire_last}.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s4">
        <h2><span class="num">4</span> Finding one — the skill curve is the baseline's diurnal phase</h2>
        <p>PM2.5 in Jakarta has a large daily cycle: on the case's own archive the mean hour runs from
        {n1(Math.min(...A.observed.diurnal.map((d: any) => d.pm25)))}&nbsp;µg/m³ in mid-afternoon to
        {n1(Math.max(...A.observed.diurnal.map((d: any) => d.pm25)))} before dawn, as the nocturnal
        boundary layer collapses and lifts. A persistence forecast at a 24-hour lead is therefore
        handed that entire cycle for free — it compares an hour to the same hour — and at a 12-hour lead
        it is handed the cycle inverted.</p>
        <p>The autocorrelation function says this precisely. It decays from
        {acfAt(1).toFixed(2)} at one hour to a trough of <b>{acfAt(12).toFixed(2)}</b> at twelve, then
        <i>rebounds</i> to <b>{acfAt(24).toFixed(2)}</b> at twenty-four, troughs again at
        {acfAt(36).toFixed(2)}, rebounds to {acfAt(48).toFixed(2)}, and so on for three full days.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F2} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Above: the autocorrelation of hourly PM2.5 across all stations,
          {A.acf[A.acf.length - 1].lag} lags, on the case's own complete hourly grid. Amber marks the
          diurnal echoes, slate the anti-phase troughs. Below: mean absolute error at the same leads.
          The model's error (cyan) rises monotonically the way a forecast's should. Persistence's
          (coral) does not — it peaks at 12 hours and falls back at 24, tracing the inverse of the
          curve above. Hover any point.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Persistence MAE at 12 hours is {n2(H[12].baselines.find((b: any) => b.key === "b_persist").mae)}&nbsp;µg/m³
        and at 24 hours it is <i>lower</i>, {n2(H[24].baselines.find((b: any) => b.key === "b_persist").mae)}.
        No forecast gets easier further out. The baseline simply got better at 24 hours because it was
        allowed to be in phase.</p>
        <p>So the published skill curve — {spct(H[6].skill_vs_persistence)} at 6 hours, peaking at
        <b>{spct(H[12].skill_vs_persistence)} at 12</b>, back to {spct(H[24].skill_vs_persistence)} at
        24 — is not describing where the model is strong. It is describing where persistence is weak.
        Replacing persistence at each lead with whichever trivial rule is actually best there removes
        the shape entirely.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F3} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> The same model, two rulers. Coral is skill against persistence,
          as the case publishes it. Cyan is skill against the strongest trivial baseline available at
          each lead — named in the panel at right, and never the model's own machinery. The peak at
          12 hours disappears; what remains is a flat band near {pct(h24.skill_vs_best, 0)} from
          6 to 24 hours, decaying at 48 and 72.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>The case's own dashboard already says the important half of this — that persistence "gets
        this cycle for free, which is exactly why it is a hard baseline to beat." It does not draw the
        conclusion, which is that a curve measured against a baseline with a phase cannot be read as a
        statement about the model.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s5">
        <h2><span class="num">5</span> Finding two — the registered gate does not survive a fairer baseline</h2>
        <p>The case's first check requires the 24-hour forecast to beat persistence by
        {pct(GR.threshold, 0)} on MAE. It reports {spct(GR.published_skill)} and passes. The threshold
        was fixed before the run and has not been moved, which is the right discipline. The question is
        whether the comparison it is applied to is the right comparison.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F4} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> The 24-hour horizon against every trivial baseline, on the
          identical {n0(h24.n)} held-out station-hours. Left: mean absolute error. Right: the model's
          skill over each, with 95% intervals from a bootstrap over whole station-days. Amber marks the
          best-performing trivial rule and the case's own {pct(GR.threshold, 0)} threshold.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Persistence is not the hardest trivial baseline at this lead. A <b>{GR.best_baseline.toLowerCase()}</b>
        — carrying forward the mean of the sensor's last 24 reported hours, which requires no model,
        no meteorology and no fire data — reaches MAE {n2(GR.best_baseline_mae)}&nbsp;µg/m³ against
        persistence's {n2(bl24.b_persist.mae)}. Against it the model's skill is
        <b>{spct(GR.skill_vs_best)}</b>, with a 95% interval of
        [{spct(GR.ci[0])},&nbsp;{spct(GR.ci[1])}].</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>The gate fails on this ruler, and not marginally.</b> The whole interval
        lies below the {pct(GR.threshold, 0)} threshold; the bootstrap puts the probability of clearing
        it at <b>{(GR.p_meets_threshold * 100).toFixed(2)}%</b>. On the case's chosen baseline the
        result is {spct(GR.published_skill)} [{spct(GR.persistence_ci[0])},&nbsp;{spct(GR.persistence_ci[1])}],
        which does clear the bar — but by {((BT.b_persist.lo - GR.threshold) * 100).toFixed(1)}
        percentage points at the lower bound, on an interval the case does not publish. A pass this
        narrow, resting this heavily on the choice of comparator, should be reported as a pass with an
        interval and a named baseline, not as a headline percentage.</p>
        <p>Two baselines are worth noting for what they say about the data rather than the model. The
        seasonal-diurnal climatology is <i>worse</i> ({n2(bl24.b_clim_seas.mae)}) than the plain diurnal
        one ({n2(bl24.b_clim_hour.mae)}), which it should not be if the seasonal cycle were stable and
        the panel were stationary — a first sign of §6. And persisting the anomaly, the meteorological
        standard, lands at {n2(bl24.b_anom.mae)}: statistically indistinguishable from plain
        persistence, because at a 24-hour lead the two are nearly the same estimator.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s6">
        <h2><span class="num">6</span> Finding three — the held-out future is a panel that dissolves</h2>
        <p>The split is placed at the 75th percentile of <i>observed</i> hours rather than of the
        calendar, and the reasoning given for that is sound: splitting the calendar would have handed
        the test set whichever months the network happened to be dead in. But it has a consequence the
        case does not follow through. Because observations are concentrated where the network was alive,
        the "final 25%" of observed hours is not the final 25% of the timeline.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F5} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Every scored 24-hour forecast in the held-out window, by month
          and station; the numerals above each bar are the count of stations scored that month. Band
          heights within a month are drawn as equal shares — the count of bands and the total height
          are the exact figures; the split between them is not published per month.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p><b>{pct(A.panel.share_before_2026, 1)}</b> of the {n0(A.panel.n_test_rows)} scored hours fall
        before 2026. The final {lastMonths.length} months of the record — the months the dashboard's
        live panel actually describes — contribute <b>{lastMonthRows} scored hours between them</b>.
        The panel falls from {A.panel.months[0].n_stations} stations in
        {A.panel.months[0].ym} to {A.panel.months[A.panel.months.length - 1].n_stations} at the end, and
        the intersection of the first month's stations and the last month's is <b>empty</b>: not one
        station is present throughout, so there is no sub-panel on which a like-for-like comparison over
        the window can be made at all.</p>
        <p>Four stations — {A.panel.by_station.slice(0, 4).map((s: any) => s.name).join(", ")} — carry
        <b>{pct(A.panel.by_station.slice(0, 4).reduce((s: number, r: any) => s + r.share, 0), 1)}</b> of
        the evaluation. Every one of them has since stopped reporting. The headline skill number is
        therefore a weighted average over a set of instruments that no longer exists, and the weights
        are set by which of them happened to survive longest, not by any sampling design.</p>
        <p class="caveat">This is the condition that <a href="#ref-rolling">the rolling-origin study</a>
        was written about. A single chronological split turned {pct(L.elche.rolling_h1, 1)} skill into
        +{pct(L.elche.static_lo, 1)} in that paper's PM10 case. We cannot rerun this case's model
        across rolling folds without refitting it, so we do not claim the same reversal here — but the
        monthly error in Figure 4 moves between {n2(Math.min(...A.panel.months.filter((m: any) => m.n_rows > 500).map((m: any) => m.model_mae)))}
        and {n2(Math.max(...A.panel.months.filter((m: any) => m.n_rows > 500).map((m: any) => m.model_mae)))}&nbsp;µg/m³
        as the panel changes shape, which is the variability a single split averages away.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s7">
        <h2><span class="num">7</span> Finding four — what the model learned, and what it cannot carry</h2>
        <p>The case's own driver check reports that the single strongest feature at 24 hours is
        <b>station identity</b>, ahead of the sensor's current reading and every meteorological term.
        The case publishes this as a failed check and reads it correctly, as "a real result about a
        sparse, heterogeneous network." It has a sharper consequence than that, and the panel contains
        an accidental experiment for it.</p>
        <p>{fresh.name} first reported on {A.panel.by_station.find((s: any) => s.location_id === fresh.location_id)?.first},
        which is <i>after</i> the training cut. It contributes {n0(fresh.n_test)} scored hours —
        {pct(A.panel.by_station.find((s: any) => s.location_id === fresh.location_id)?.share ?? 0, 1)} of
        the evaluation — and the model has <b>never seen it</b>. It is, unintentionally, the only honest
        estimate on offer of what this forecast would do at a newly installed sensor.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F6} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Every station in the held-out window. Left: hours available in
          training. Centre: Europe's forecast quality indicator — the model's RMSE divided by
          persistence's, which must sit at or below 1.00. Right: how much of the observations the
          model's nominal 80% prediction interval actually contains. Amber is the station the model
          never trained on; coral is the station that fails the indicator.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>At the station it never saw, skill against persistence is <b>{spct(fresh.skill_persist)}</b>
        against a mean of {spct(seenSkill)} across the stations it trained on, and its 80% interval
        covers <b>{pct(fresh.pi80, 0)}</b> against {pct(seenPI, 0)}. Because station identity is the
        model's strongest lever, most of what looks like forecasting skill is per-sensor calibration
        that cannot leave the sensor it was learned on. That matters directly: the operational proposal
        implied by any city nowcast is that new monitors get forecasts, and this is the number that
        would apply to them.</p>
        <p>Applying the European criterion to the panel as a whole: <b>{FM.n_pass} of
        {FM.n_stations}</b> stations reach MQI ≤ {L.fmqo.threshold.toFixed(2)}, which is
        {pct(FM.share_pass, 1)} against the {pct(L.fmqo.station_share, 0)} required. <b>The case does
        not meet Europe's acceptance criterion for an operational air-quality forecast</b>, and the one
        station that fails it — MQI {FM.worst_mqi.toFixed(2)}, more than twice persistence's error — is
        {FM.worst}, the only station in Jabodetabek still reporting. §10 explains why.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s8">
        <h2><span class="num">8</span> Finding five — the forecast cannot make the calls it exists to make</h2>
        <p>A nowcast earns its keep on bad days. The case tests this at one threshold,
        {L.standards.us_unhealthy}&nbsp;µg/m³, reports recall {rung(55.5).recall.toFixed(3)} at
        precision {rung(55.5).precision.toFixed(3)}, and publishes the check as failed against its
        {(0.5).toFixed(2)} target. That is honest, and it understates the problem, because the US air
        quality index has two rungs above that one and Indonesia's own daily limit sits just below it —
        <b>{L.standards.id_daily}&nbsp;µg/m³</b> under {L.standards.id_ref}, within
        {(L.standards.us_unhealthy - L.standards.id_daily).toFixed(1)}&nbsp;µg/m³ of the threshold
        already tested.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F7} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Above: how far the observations reach against how far the
          forecast reaches, on the same concentration axis, with the US AQI categories behind them.
          Solid to the 99th percentile, faint to the maximum. Below: at each category, the hours that
          happened and the hours the forecast called.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Across {n0(h24.n)} held-out hours the model's highest 24-hour prediction is
        <b>{n1(ceil24.pred_max)}&nbsp;µg/m³</b>. The observed maximum is <b>{n1(ceil24.obs_max)}</b>.
        There were <b>{n0(rung(125.5).n_observed)}</b> hours at or above the "very unhealthy" line and
        <b>{n0(rung(225.5).n_observed)}</b> at or above "hazardous"; the forecast issued
        <b>{rung(125.5).n_predicted}</b> and <b>{rung(225.5).n_predicted}</b> respectively. Recall at
        both is <b>exactly zero</b>, not by bad luck but by construction: a model trained on log1p and
        optimised for absolute error shrinks towards the middle of its conditional distribution, and
        this one has drawn a ceiling at roughly {n1(ceil24.pred_max)}&nbsp;µg/m³ that no input
        combination crosses.</p>
        <p>The same shrinkage shows up as sign-flipped bias. On the {n0(EP.episode.n)} episode hours
        the model runs <b>{n1(EP.episode.bias)}&nbsp;µg/m³</b> low; on the {n0(EP.clean.n)} clean hours
        it runs <b>+{n1(EP.clean.bias)}</b> high. It is systematically optimistic exactly when being
        optimistic costs something.</p>
        <p>This is not peculiar to this implementation. <a href="#ref-wangdu">Wang &amp; Du</a> report
        that on their extreme-concentration subset, <i>persistence beat every machine-learning model
        they tested at 6 and 24 hours</i>. {L.beijing.extreme_note} A pooled error metric averages the
        episode failure into a large majority of ordinary hours and reports the average as skill.</p>
        <p class="caveat">The 80% prediction interval is the other half of this. It covers
        {pct(G["G-E5"].value, 1)} of observations against a 72–88% requirement, and the case publishes
        the failure. <a href="#ref-murad">Murad et al.</a> document precisely this failure mode for
        precisely this estimator: quantile-regression gradient boosting delivering
        {pct(L.trondheim.picp_qgb, 0)} coverage on a nominal {pct(L.trondheim.picp_nominal_qgb, 0)}
        interval for PM2.5, where Bayesian neural networks on the same data reached
        {pct(L.trondheim.picp_bnn_lo, 0)}–{pct(L.trondheim.picp_bnn_hi, 0)}. The band is not
        irreparable; it is the wrong estimator for the job.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s9">
        <h2><span class="num">9</span> Finding six — the error floor belongs to the instrument, not the model</h2>
        <p>Every error figure above is measured against readings from sensors, and OpenAQ pools
        regulatory-grade monitors with consumer hardware under one field name. In this bbox that
        distinction is large and it is measurable.</p>
        <div class="stat-row">
          <div class="stat"><span class="sv">{n1(prov["AirGradient"].mean)}</span><span class="sl">µg/m³ mean across {prov["AirGradient"].n_stations} consumer-grade stations ({n0(prov["AirGradient"].n_hours)} h)</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="sv">{n1(prov["AirNow"].mean)}</span><span class="sl">µg/m³ mean across the {prov["AirNow"].n_stations} regulatory monitors ({n0(prov["AirNow"].n_hours)} h)</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="sv accent">+{pct(prov["AirGradient"].mean / prov["AirNow"].mean - 1, 0)}</span><span class="sl">what the consumer units read above the regulatory ones</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>The case's headline air-quality figure — a mean of
        {n1(A.thresholds.station_hour_mean)}&nbsp;µg/m³ — is a station-hour average over that mixed
        panel. Restricted to the regulatory-grade subset it is
        <b>{n1(A.providers.reference_mean)}</b>, which lands inside the published range for the city:
        <a href="#ref-iqair">IQAir</a> reports Jakarta at {n1(L.jakarta_level.iqair_2023)} for 2023 and
        {n1(L.jakarta_level.iqair_2024)} for 2024, and a
        <a href="#ref-aogh">peer-reviewed Jabodetabek study</a> reports {n1(L.jakarta_level.peer_jabodetabek)}.
        The published number is roughly
        {pct(A.thresholds.station_hour_mean / A.providers.reference_mean - 1, 0)} above its own
        reference-grade subset, and the difference is instrument class, not geography. (We checked the
        obvious alternative explanation and it is not that: weighting stations equally rather than by
        hour moves the mean only from {n1(A.thresholds.station_hour_mean)} to
        {n1(A.thresholds.station_balanced_mean)}.)</p>
        <p>Two of these instruments happen to sit at the same address.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F8} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> {CO.a_name} ({CO.a_provider}) against {CO.b_name}
          ({CO.b_provider}), {CO.m} metres apart, every hour both reported. Dashed line is exact
          agreement. Inset compares that disagreement with the model's own forecast error and with the
          published uncorrected error of this sensor model against a reference instrument in a
          comparable tropical climate. Hover any point.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Over {n0(CO.n)} shared hours the two disagree by <b>{n2(CO.mae)}&nbsp;µg/m³</b> on average
        (RMSE {n2(CO.rmse)}, correlation {CO.r.toFixed(3)}). That is the observing system's own error at
        zero distance and zero lead time, and no forecast can be scored below it. The model's entire
        <i>one-hour</i> error is {n2(H[1].model_mae)}&nbsp;µg/m³ — only
        <b>{n2(H[1].model_mae - CO.mae)}</b> above the floor. At 24 hours,
        {n2(CO.rmse)}²⁄{n2(H[24].model_rmse)}² of the mean squared error —
        about {pct((CO.rmse / H[24].model_rmse) ** 2, 0)} — is target noise rather than anything a
        model could predict.</p>
        <p>The literature makes the floor worse, not better. <a href="#ref-raheja">Raheja et al. (2023)</a>
        co-located this exact sensor family against a {L.accra.reference_instrument} reference in Accra,
        a tropical high-humidity city, and found uncorrected MAE of
        <b>{n1(L.accra.clarity_mae_raw)}&nbsp;µg/m³</b> (R² {L.accra.clarity_r2_raw}, slope
        {L.accra.clarity_slope_raw}, over {L.accra.rh_lo}–{L.accra.rh_hi}% relative humidity), falling
        to {n1(L.accra.clarity_mae_corrected)} after calibration. The same hardware in semi-arid
        Lubbock gives {n1(L.lubbock.clarity_mae_raw)}: the {(L.accra.clarity_mae_raw / L.lubbock.clarity_mae_raw).toFixed(1)}×
        degradation is the humidity regime, and Jakarta's regime is Accra's. Uncorrected, that error is
        <b>{pct(L.accra.clarity_mae_raw / e24.model_mae, 0)}</b> of the model's entire 24-hour forecast
        error.</p>
        <p class="caveat">None of this makes the model bad. It makes the reported error only weakly
        attributable to the model. Before the next tenth of a µg/m³ of forecast skill is worth chasing,
        the sensors need a correction function — and a correction function needs a reference monitor,
        which brings us to the real finding.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s10">
        <h2><span class="num">10</span> Finding seven — the last witness, and why sparsity is the result</h2>
        <p>Start with the denominator. The case reports {A.network.n_stations_total} PM2.5 stations in
        the box, {A.network.n_live} of them live. The registry is noisier than that. Those
        {A.network.n_stations_total} registrations resolve to <b>{A.addresses.n_addresses} distinct
        addresses</b> — {A.addresses.largest_cluster.n} of them are the same coordinate carrying
        {A.addresses.largest_cluster.names.slice(0, 2).map((s: string) => `"${s}"`).join(" and ")} and
        four more names, none of which ever returned a reading. <b>{A.addresses.n_never_reported} of the
        {A.addresses.n_registered} have never delivered an hour of data</b>, including two legacy
        diplomatic-post entries duplicating feeds that are separately registered. The honest count is
        <b>{A.addresses.n_ever_reported} instruments that ever reported</b>, of which
        {A.network.n_live === 2 ? "two are flagged live and one of those has delivered no hours at all" : "few remain"}.</p>
        <p class="pull">Told as "{A.network.n_live} of {A.network.n_stations_total}", this reads as an
        access problem. Told as "{A.addresses.n_ever_reported} instruments ever worked and
        {A.panel.months[A.panel.months.length - 1].n_stations} is reporting now", it is a
        capability problem — and told properly, it is not about measurement at all.</p>
        <p>The two co-located instruments of §9 were tracking each other closely — monthly means within
        a few µg/m³, both catching the December wet-season drop together — for fourteen months. Then
        they stopped.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F9} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 8.</b> Monthly mean PM2.5 at the two co-located instruments, computed
          only on the hours <i>both</i> reported, so the comparison is never confounded by different
          sampling. The shaded region is where they cease to agree; the small figures under it are how
          many shared hours each of those months rests on. {CO.b_name} went silent on {CO.last};
          {CO.a_name} is the station the dashboard's live panel now describes.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>From <b>{coAfter[0]?.ym}</b> the two diverge and never reconverge. In the last month both
        were alive, on the hours both reported, one read <b>{n1(coLast.a)}</b> and the other
        <b>{n1(coLast.b)}</b>&nbsp;µg/m³ — a factor of {(coLast.b / coLast.a).toFixed(1)}, at
        {CO.m} metres. Then the second instrument went dark. It is the surviving one that reads low,
        and its held-out quality indicator is {FM.worst_mqi.toFixed(2)} — the worst in the panel, and
        the reason the network as a whole fails the European criterion.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>How much this rests on.</b> The {coBefore.length} months of agreement are
        built on {n0(coBeforeHours)} shared hours; the {coAfter.length} months of divergence on only
        <b>{coAfterHours}</b>, because the surviving instrument's own reporting collapsed at the same
        time. That is thin, and we say so. But the divergence is not a sampling artefact: the two
        readings are drawn from identical hours by construction, the gap is
        {n1(coLast.b - coLast.a)}&nbsp;µg/m³ against a pooled agreement of {n2(CO.mae)}, and it holds
        in the same direction in every one of those months. It is enough to say the surviving
        instrument needs checking; it is not enough to say which of the two was right — and there is
        no longer anything in Jabodetabek that could settle it.</p>
        <p>We cannot say which of the two is right. That is the point. <b>A network of one cannot be
        wrong, because nothing can contradict it.</b> The value of a monitoring network is not that it
        measures more places; it is that instruments check each other, and a single number that no other
        number can dispute is not evidence. What Jabodetabek has lost is not coverage. It is
        falsifiability.</p>
        <p>For scale: the European framework directive requires
        <b>{L.standards.eu_points_per_million} PM2.5 urban-background sampling point per million
        inhabitants</b> summed over agglomerations ({L.standards.eu_ref}), which for
        {L.standards.jabodetabek_millions} million is roughly <b>{euPoints}</b>. The US federal minimum
        is flatter and does not scale with size — {L.standards.epa_min_large_msa} sites for any
        metropolitan area above a million ({L.standards.epa_ref}) — and Jabodetabek does not currently
        meet that either: the case's own coverage check finds <b>{G["G-E4"].value}</b> stations at 80%
        completeness over the trailing 90 days, against a peak of {peakCov} reached in 2024.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F1} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 9.</b> The observing system across the whole record. Cyan is stations
          returning any PM2.5 that month; teal and amber are those clearing 50% and 80% hourly
          completeness. The coral rule is the {L.standards.epa_min_large_msa}-site US federal minimum
          for any metropolitan area above a million people ({L.standards.epa_ref}); the European
          per-million rule would put the requirement near {euPoints}. The network peaks at
          {Math.max(...A.panel.coverage.map((c: any) => c.n_any))} in 2024 and ends at
          {A.panel.coverage[A.panel.coverage.length - 1].n_any}. It does not decline for a reason
          anyone published; it simply stops.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="s11">
        <h2><span class="num">11</span> What follows for decisions</h2>
        <p>Evidence is only worth gathering if it changes an action. Read strictly, this work supports
        three uses and forbids a fourth.</p>
        <ol class="uses">
          <li><b>An instrumentation case, addressed to the people who fund instruments.</b> The
          arithmetic in §10 is the product: {A.addresses.n_ever_reported} instruments ever worked, one
          reports now, and the last independent check available said the survivor and its neighbour
          differed by a factor of {(coLast.b / coLast.a).toFixed(1)}.
          For the Ministry of Environment and the DKI Jakarta environment agency, that converts an
          abstract "we need more monitors" into a costed, falsifiable claim with a European benchmark
          ({euPoints} points) and a US floor ({L.standards.epa_min_large_msa}) attached to it. A
          reference-grade monitor is the cheapest thing on this list and it unlocks everything
          else.</li>
          <li><b>Sensor audit and correction.</b> The co-location result is a working method, not a
          finding to file. Two instruments at one address, {n0(CO.n)} shared hours, and the divergence
          is dated to the month. Run deliberately against one reference monitor, the same procedure
          yields the correction functions the consumer network needs — and
          <a href="#ref-raheja">the published Accra result</a> says correction takes this hardware from
          {n1(L.accra.clarity_mae_raw)} to {n1(L.accra.clarity_mae_corrected)}&nbsp;µg/m³, which is a
          larger improvement than any forecasting gain in this entire review.</li>
          <li><b>Climatological planning, not next-day operations.</b> The seasonal shape is robust and
          is the part of this that survives every test here: {n1(Math.min(...A.observed.monthly.map((m: any) => m.pm25)))}&nbsp;µg/m³
          in the wet-season trough against {n1(Math.max(...A.observed.monthly.map((m: any) => m.pm25)))}
          at the peak, and a daily cycle worth
          {(Math.max(...A.observed.diurnal.map((d: any) => d.pm25)) / Math.min(...A.observed.diurnal.map((d: any) => d.pm25))).toFixed(1)}×.
          For BMKG, for school-calendar planning, and for scheduling outdoor work, a defensible seasonal
          and diurnal climatology is available now and needs no model.</li>
          <li class="no"><b>Not: next-day episode warnings.</b> The forecast called
          {rung(125.5).n_predicted} of {n0(rung(125.5).n_observed)} "very unhealthy" hours and
          {rung(225.5).n_predicted} of {n0(rung(225.5).n_observed)} "hazardous" ones. Its interval
          covers {pct(G["G-E5"].value, 0)} where it claims 80%. Its skill over a trivial rule is
          {spct(GR.skill_vs_best)} at 24 hours, on a panel that no longer exists. No school advisory or
          outdoor-work suspension should be triggered by it in its current form, and the page should say
          so in those words.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="pull">The most valuable output of this pipeline is not the forecast. It is the
        evidence that Greater Jakarta can no longer check its own air — produced, unavoidably, by
        trying to forecast it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s12">
        <h2><span class="num">12</span> What remains open</h2>
        <p>Three things this review could not settle, stated as work not done rather than as caveats.</p>
        <ol class="next">
          <li><b>A rolling-origin re-evaluation.</b> Everything in §5 and §6 points the same way, but
          settling it requires refitting the model across expanding folds rather than one cut, which is
          the single highest-value next experiment and the one the
          <a href="#ref-rolling">PM10 study</a> shows can reverse a sign.</li>
          <li><b>The fire tail is unexplained and unverifiable.</b> {A.fire.peak_month} carries
          {n0(A.fire.peak_n)} hotspots across the airshed — <b>{A.fire.ratio_to_median.toFixed(0)}×</b>
          the median month and {(A.fire.peak_n / A.fire.prev_max_n).toFixed(1)}× the previous record
          ({A.fire.prev_max_month}, the strong El Niño season). It is geographically coherent rather
          than scattered, which argues against a simple processing fault, but it sits entirely in the
          near-real-time product, it feeds the model as a live input, and it arrives in the one month
          the ground network delivered {augObsHours.obs_hours} observed hours. We decline to adjudicate
          it and recommend the case does not present it as an established fire season either.</li>
          <li><b>Recalibrate the interval before anything else.</b> The point forecast is defensible for
          climatological use; the band is not, at {pct(G["G-E5"].value, 1)} coverage. The literature
          names both the failure mode and estimators that do not have it.</li>
        </ol>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F10} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 10.</b> Monthly VIIRS hotspot counts across the airshed (bars, left
          axis) against ground PM2.5 hours actually observed (cyan, right axis). The largest smoke
          signal in the record arrives at the moment the city has almost no capacity to measure whether
          it reached the ground.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

